{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015f8QyCnDPmyQHN5bQGJ4rd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\trole: |\n2\t    You are a software architect planning work for an AI code generation pipeline. Each task you propose will be executed by a separate Claude instance (the \"stitch agent\") that sees only its task description and the project rules. The stitch agent has no memory of this conversation and no access to your analysis.\n3\tproject_context:\n4\t    vision:\n5\t        file: docs/VISION.yaml\n6\t        id: go-unix-utils\n7\t        title: 'go-unix-utils: Specification-Driven Regeneration of Unix Utilities in Go'\n8\t        executive_summary: |\n9\t            go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix\n10\t            utilities as the test bed. We derive formal specifications from C source code, generate\n11\t            Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against\n12\t            reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is not to ship utilities; it\n13\t            is to find where the methodology breaks down.\n14\t        problem: |\n15\t            Classic Unix utilities have no formal specifications accessible to implementers. Man\n16\t            pages omit edge cases, flag interactions, and exit-code contracts. Re-implementations\n17\t            built from documentation alone diverge in observable behavior -- they break scripts,\n18\t            pipelines, and tools that depend on precise semantics.\n19\t\n20\t            Existing Go re-implementations treat utilities as isolated exercises. They lack shared\n21\t            infrastructure, behavioral contracts, and systematic verification. The result is\n22\t            utilities that work for common cases but fail on edge cases that production users\n23\t            encounter.\n24\t        what_this_does: |\n25\t            We run a four-stage pipeline for each utility. In the extraction stage, we clone the\n26\t            C source (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils) and analyze behavior directly from\n27\t            the source of truth. In the specification stage, a human operator uses the make-work\n28\t            and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two artifacts: a SRD (numbered requirements\n29\t            and acceptance criteria covering all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors) and a\n30\t            use case (concrete end-to-end execution path through the system). Shared library\n31\t            components (pkg/) also receive SRDs when their requirements span multiple utilities.\n32\t            In the synthesis stage, the mage-claude-orchestrator library drives a Claude agent to\n33\t            generate idiomatic Go from the SRD. In the verification stage, a differential testing\n34\t            harness runs the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary side by side and compares\n35\t            outputs.\n36\t        why_we_build_this: |\n37\t            We use GNU utilities as a stress test for the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation\n38\t            system. GNU utilities are an ideal subject: their behavior is authoritative (C source\n39\t            is public), their correctness is verifiable (differential testing against reference\n40\t            binaries), and they span a wide range of complexity -- from ts (trivial) to ls\n41\t            (hundreds of flags and edge cases). The human operator guides spec development through\n42\t            make-work and do-work skills; the orchestrator then takes over and generates Go code\n43\t            autonomously. We want to know where the system holds and where it fails: how much\n44\t            spec detail the orchestrator needs, how it handles complex flag interactions, and\n45\t            whether differential testing catches the gaps. The shared Go library (pkg/) is a\n46\t            by-product, not the goal.\n47\t        success_criteria:\n48\t            SC1: |\n49\t                Every utility passes differential tests against its Homebrew reference binary with 100% output parity on exit code, stdout, and stderr.\n50\t            SC2: |\n51\t                Every utility has a SRD that covers all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors derived from C source.\n52\t            SC3: |\n53\t                Go binaries are idiomatic and compile with no warnings under standard linting (golangci-lint).\n54\t            SC4: |\n55\t                The shared pkg/ library is used by at least two utilities for common logic, with no duplication of syscall or formatting code across cmd/ packages.\n56\t            SC5: mage analyze passes with no broken references or missing fields.\n57\t            SC6: |\n58\t                We can characterize where mage-claude-orchestrator succeeds and fails: which utility complexity levels it handles autonomously, which require spec refinement by the human operator, and which it cannot complete without manual intervention.\n59\t        implementation_phases:\n60\t            - phase: \"1\"\n61\t              focus: 'Foundation: pipeline infrastructure, first utility (ts), shared pkg/ skeleton.'\n62\t              deliverables: Differential testing harness in pkg/testutils/, pkg/sys/ and pkg/format/ initial implementations, cmd/ts/ with SRD and passing differential tests.\n63\t            - phase: \"2\"\n64\t              focus: 'Moreutils: remaining moreutils tools (sponge, vidir, pee, chronic).'\n65\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for sponge, vidir, pee, and chronic. Differential tests for each moreutils utility.\n66\t            - phase: \"3\"\n67\t              focus: 'Coreutils: high-complexity coreutils tools (ls, cat, cp, mv, wc, du).'\n68\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for each coreutils utility. Shared formatting and syscall logic promoted to pkg/.\n69\t            - phase: \"4\"\n70\t              focus: 'Search Utilities: grep, egrep, fgrep (grep package) and find, xargs (findutils).'\n71\t              deliverables: SRD and Go implementations for all grep and findutils tools. Full differential test coverage for the search utility suite.\n72\t        risks:\n73\t            - risk: Platform divergence between GNU and macOS behavior.\n74\t              impact: high\n75\t              likelihood: medium\n76\t              mitigation: |\n77\t                We target Homebrew-installed GNU binaries (gls, gts, etc.) as the reference,\n78\t                not the macOS BSD built-ins. All tests run against the GNU reference binary,\n79\t                which is built from the same C source we use for spec extraction.\n80\t            - risk: C source complexity makes full behavioral extraction impractical for some utilities.\n81\t              impact: medium\n82\t              likelihood: medium\n83\t              mitigation: |\n84\t                We combine C source analysis with behavioral testing of the reference binary\n85\t                for edge cases. Specs note where behavior is observed rather than source-derived,\n86\t                and we include those observations as test inputs in the differential harness.\n87\t            - risk: Go standard library lacks POSIX syscall coverage on macOS.\n88\t              impact: medium\n89\t              likelihood: low\n90\t              mitigation: |\n91\t                We build pkg/sys/ as an abstraction layer over Darwin and Linux syscalls and\n92\t                use golang.org/x/sys for extended coverage where the standard library falls short.\n93\t            - risk: AI synthesis produces code that passes common cases but fails edge cases.\n94\t              impact: high\n95\t              likelihood: medium\n96\t              mitigation: |\n97\t                Differential testing is the gating check. Code cannot pass review until\n98\t                differential tests pass. Edge cases are explicit in the SRD and exercised\n99\t                as named test inputs in pkg/testutils/.\n100\t            - risk: Go has no locale-aware string collation (strcoll equivalent)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4358f053-a4e0-454a-b89d-ccb080f1a675","uuid":"faeb3b70-2083-434e-8f78-ad5742c20a4f","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:55:30.086Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5224/.cobbler/history/2026-05-14-18-46-04-measure-prompt.yaml","content":"role: |\n    You are a software architect planning work for an AI code generation pipeline. Each task you propose will be executed by a separate Claude instance (the \"stitch agent\") that sees only its task description and the project rules. The stitch agent has no memory of this conversation and no access to your analysis.\nproject_context:\n    vision:\n        file: docs/VISION.yaml\n        id: go-unix-utils\n        title: 'go-unix-utils: Specification-Driven Regeneration of Unix Utilities in Go'\n        executive_summary: |\n            go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix\n            utilities as the test bed. We derive formal specifications from C source code, generate\n            Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against\n            reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is not to ship utilities; it\n            is to find where the methodology breaks down.\n        problem: |\n            Classic Unix utilities have no formal specifications accessible to implementers. Man\n            pages omit edge cases, flag interactions, and exit-code contracts. Re-implementations\n            built from documentation alone diverge in observable behavior -- they break scripts,\n            pipelines, and tools that depend on precise semantics.\n\n            Existing Go re-implementations treat utilities as isolated exercises. They lack shared\n            infrastructure, behavioral contracts, and systematic verification. The result is\n            utilities that work for common cases but fail on edge cases that production users\n            encounter.\n        what_this_does: |\n            We run a four-stage pipeline for each utility. In the extraction stage, we clone the\n            C source (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils) and analyze behavior directly from\n            the source of truth. In the specification stage, a human operator uses the make-work\n            and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two artifacts: a SRD (numbered requirements\n            and acceptance criteria covering all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors) and a\n            use case (concrete end-to-end execution path through the system). Shared library\n            components (pkg/) also receive SRDs when their requirements span multiple utilities.\n            In the synthesis stage, the mage-claude-orchestrator library drives a Claude agent to\n            generate idiomatic Go from the SRD. In the verification stage, a differential testing\n            harness runs the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary side by side and compares\n            outputs.\n        why_we_build_this: |\n            We use GNU utilities as a stress test for the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation\n            system. GNU utilities are an ideal subject: their behavior is authoritative (C source\n            is public), their correctness is verifiable (differential testing against reference\n            binaries), and they span a wide range of complexity -- from ts (trivial) to ls\n            (hundreds of flags and edge cases). The human operator guides spec development through\n            make-work and do-work skills; the orchestrator then takes over and generates Go code\n            autonomously. We want to know where the system holds and where it fails: how much\n            spec detail the orchestrator needs, how it handles complex flag interactions, and\n            whether differential testing catches the gaps. The shared Go library (pkg/) is a\n            by-product, not the goal.\n        success_criteria:\n            SC1: |\n                Every utility passes differential tests against its Homebrew reference binary with 100% output parity on exit code, stdout, and stderr.\n            SC2: |\n                Every utility has a SRD that covers all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors derived from C source.\n            SC3: |\n                Go binaries are idiomatic and compile with no warnings under standard linting (golangci-lint).\n            SC4: |\n                The shared pkg/ library is used by at least two utilities for common logic, with no duplication of syscall or formatting code across cmd/ packages.\n            SC5: mage analyze passes with no broken references or missing fields.\n            SC6: |\n                We can characterize where mage-claude-orchestrator succeeds and fails: which utility complexity levels it handles autonomously, which require spec refinement by the human operator, and which it cannot complete without manual intervention.\n        implementation_phases:\n            - phase: \"1\"\n              focus: 'Foundation: pipeline infrastructure, first utility (ts), shared pkg/ skeleton.'\n              deliverables: Differential testing harness in pkg/testutils/, pkg/sys/ and pkg/format/ initial implementations, cmd/ts/ with SRD and passing differential tests.\n            - phase: \"2\"\n              focus: 'Moreutils: remaining moreutils tools (sponge, vidir, pee, chronic).'\n              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for sponge, vidir, pee, and chronic. Differential tests for each moreutils utility.\n            - phase: \"3\"\n              focus: 'Coreutils: high-complexity coreutils tools (ls, cat, cp, mv, wc, du).'\n              deliverables: SRD and Go implementation for each coreutils utility. Shared formatting and syscall logic promoted to pkg/.\n            - phase: \"4\"\n              focus: 'Search Utilities: grep, egrep, fgrep (grep package) and find, xargs (findutils).'\n              deliverables: SRD and Go implementations for all grep and findutils tools. Full differential test coverage for the search utility suite.\n        risks:\n            - risk: Platform divergence between GNU and macOS behavior.\n              impact: high\n              likelihood: medium\n              mitigation: |\n                We target Homebrew-installed GNU binaries (gls, gts, etc.) as the reference,\n                not the macOS BSD built-ins. All tests run against the GNU reference binary,\n                which is built from the same C source we use for spec extraction.\n            - risk: C source complexity makes full behavioral extraction impractical for some utilities.\n              impact: medium\n              likelihood: medium\n              mitigation: |\n                We combine C source analysis with behavioral testing of the reference binary\n                for edge cases. Specs note where behavior is observed rather than source-derived,\n                and we include those observations as test inputs in the differential harness.\n            - risk: Go standard library lacks POSIX syscall coverage on macOS.\n              impact: medium\n              likelihood: low\n              mitigation: |\n                We build pkg/sys/ as an abstraction layer over Darwin and Linux syscalls and\n                use golang.org/x/sys for extended coverage where the standard library falls short.\n            - risk: AI synthesis produces code that passes common cases but fails edge cases.\n              impact: high\n              likelihood: medium\n              mitigation: |\n                Differential testing is the gating check. Code cannot pass review until\n                differential tests pass. Edge cases are explicit in the SRD and exercised\n                as named test inputs in pkg/testutils/.\n            - risk: Go has no locale-aware string collation (strcoll equivalent).","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":4907}}}
